In article <39cd97e3$0$35021$2c3edae7@news.voyager.net>, Jack Patteeuw 
<jjpatteeuw@voyager.net> wrote:

> BBR on all (???) SCSI drives is handled in the firmware of the drive. 
> If the OS can't read it, its too late now.

Drives will attempt to reread bad blocks on their own, but won't remap 
them.  And retrying more times than the drive defaults to will sometimes 
work.  It isn't necessarily too late.



In article <pshqss4blaucqhka02jucjkiboes86n2ie@4ax.com>, Richard 
<dr_vmsREMV@techieREMV.com> wrote:

> Does T64 have any sort of BBR (bad block
> relocation) similar to what VMS has that will mark a particular LBN as 
> bad and
> ignore it?  

You can do it manually.  Use the scu utility's "verify media" command to 
determine which block number is bad.  The use the "reassign" command in 
scu to remap the block.  man scu is not too helpful, use the built-in 
"help" command of the scu program, which gives more details about the 
available commands and options.

My own advice is to get rid of any disk that develops bad blocks.  In my 
experience, nine times out of ten, a week or so later more bad blocks 
will pop up and soon the disk will have to be replaced anyway.  Better 
to replace it now and avoid the hassle.

