In article <39C7AEBE.8E037CA3@nospam.guacamole.com>, Marble Head <Marble.Head@nospam.guacamole.com> writes:

>What does it mean to be "sparse files?"

File which don't have blocks allocated to potions of the file.  Such
potions of the file read back as NULs.  Writing to these area causes
blocks to be allocated.  They created by seeking past the part of a
file where blocks are allocated and writing at the new location.  The
space in between (when large enough) won't have blocks allocated.

Some applications consider this to be a feature.  Some don't.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




