srlnrow@read.the.sig writes:

>I'm not sure I understand what you're saying here. By "Solaris 7 no longer
>accepts them" do you mean that's the cause of the "Value too large for defined
>data type" error message and that's why the file with the negative time
>is not seen in the ls -l listing on the Sun?

Yes.  When Solaris 7 started using 64 bit timestamps they couldn't just
copy the 32 bit unsigned timestamp from over the wire into the 64 bit
timestamp.

If you run a 64 bit kernel, you can use a 64 bit ls but 32 bit
apps will not so those files.


>The file is being created by whatever code is in gzip - the source of which
>was www.sunfreeware.com. Both version 1.2.4 and the trial 1.2.4.4294967298
>behave the same. I'm not enough of a C programmer to read the code with
>much understanding.

Ok, so what NFS commands fly over the wire.

>Mounting the Tru64 filesystem on the Sun with NFS v2 seems to make the problem
>go away...!?


Ah; hm.  The NFS creat protocol is different between v2 and v3;
the fact that gzip uses O_CREAT|O_EXCL causes it to use new parts
of the V3 protocol and this includes some form of a cookie on create.

Perhaps this confuses the Tru64 unix server but it could of course
also be Sun's fault.

Whetever you may think of the 32 bit timestamp problem, files with negative
timestamps should not spring into existance in normal practice; so something
else is wrong too.

Casper
