I'm relatively sure that if you set up a connection through a serial or parallel
port, and don't use a protocol such as ppp, you will be exposing yourself to a
high probability for data corruption.  You've got to use some protocol supporting
flow control and error detection/correction.

Here's another way though, which doesn't use ppp:
With Win NT, get a terminal app such as Tera Term Pro, which supports zmodem
transfers.
With UNIX, do a getty 9600 ttyS0
Connect the two via cross-over (laplink) cable from rs232 to rs232 port.  ttyS0 to
com1
Open a connection from the NT box through the COM1 or whatever.  If you tap enter
a couple of times, you should get a login prompt.
Assuming you somehow installed sz and rz properly on unix, type in "sz myfile" and
click "File, Transfer, Zmodem, Receive" on your term.  Transfer will happen.
zmodem is less efficient than ftp, but for ftp you need tcp/ip, which needs ppp
when on a serial line.

P.S. the device "ttyS0" is the naming convetion for the first serial port, in
linux.  I don't know the naming convention in unix.  Also, sz and rz are easily
ported to linux, but I don't know about unix.

