You've almost certainly corrupted your stack. With so little information, I
can only do so much random guessing about how you might have done it, but
I can offer a few tips. The places to start looking are writing through an
uninitialized pointer, using out of scope shared data, (especially when one
thread tries to share data on another thread's stack), or a misunderstanding
of some API. The possibilities, unfortunately, are almost endless.

Specifically, (though this may not help you much), you've very likely
overwritten the saved RA (r26) of a stack frame, sending the thread into
"hyperspace" when that frame tries to return. A great way to do this is to
write (or cause an API to write) into a short data type via a pointer that
was cast as if it pointed to a larger type. I used to see this a lot by
people who tried to return the "void*" value of a pthread_join into an "int
value", for example, carefully casting "&value" (pointer to a 32-bit value)
to "(void**)&value" (pointer to a 64-bit value) to keep the compiler from
complaining. This can easily happen a lot in code that was originally written
using some of the bad assumptions people learn on 32-bit systems, but is less
likely from people with some awareness of 64-bit programming. (Which is
probably why I haven't seen this one for a long time.)

