Do a "ps aux" and you will probably find that the processes are in a U state.
Signals are only delivered to a process during a context switch or on a system call
boundary so if the process is stuck in the kernel waiting for a resource then you
won't be able to kill it. A reboot is the only cure.

Stephen.


