In article <8plkfg$8fu$1@venus.telepac.pt>, "User" <user@tp.telepac.pt> 
wrote:


> I�m a newbie to Digital Unix, how can I increase the swap area?


Depending on what you're doing you might not need to worry about 
increasing the swap.  

For example, if the amount of swap you have is about the same, or less 
than, the amount of RAM, you may get these messages if the system pages 
out a lot of processes so that it can free up RAM to use as a disk 
cache.  In that case the machine may say it's getting low on swap, but 
it's not in danger of running out of memory because it could very easily 
decrease its disk buffers without significantly harming performance.  I 
see this behavior when I copy large files... it swaps out my inactive 
xterm windows and increases the disk buffers.  But I'm not repeatedly 
accessing these files, so the caching is pointless, and I don't worry if 
a low swap space message pops up.


You said you have 879 megs of swap.  So if you had, say, 768 megs of RAM 
and a lot of idle processes with lots of allocated memory, I wouldn't be 
too surprised if the machine got low on swap while trying to maximize 
its disk buffers during intesive I/O.

