> than 256?  I assume not, but if it is possible it would be nice to know.  

[...] You haven't posted the most important information: The supported
and configured visual list! This hardware apparently supports different
colour depths. Now it depends on the configuration which color depth
(or visual) is the default. Many (older) programs programs can only work
with the default visual. If your default visual is 8-bit PseudoColor, you
still run into "out of color" problems. Newer programs either look for
the most appropriate visual for themselves or can be told which visual
to use. For instance netscape has the command line option "-visual"
to dell it which visual to use. So if your hardware supports multiple
visuals and the default is PSeudoColors, your best bet is to start 
netscape with "netscape -visual xx" when XX is the visual number
of some TrueColor visual as listed in "xdpyinfo".

BTW: The default visual can be changed with

X -vclass TrueColor -depth 16

I recommend a default depth of 16 because everything higher requires 
too much memory for pixmaps and does not pay you much in terms of 
the number of available colors.

All this can be set in /etc/dt/config/Xservers (copy from 
/usr/dt/config/Xservers if it doesn't exist yet).

Tom
